Part B: Using Zenmap (GUI)
Step‑1: Launch Zenmap from the Start Menu (Windows), Applications menu (Linux), or Applications folder (macOS).
Step‑2: In the Target field, enter the target to scan (e.g., 192.168.1.1, example.com, 192.168.1.0/24).
Step‑3: In the Profile dropdown, select a scan profile:

Profile	Description
Intense scan	Comprehensive scan: OS detection, version detection, script scanning, traceroute (aggressive timing)
Ping scan	Only detects live hosts (no port scanning)
Quick scan	Faster scan with aggressive timing and common ports
Regular scan	Standard Nmap scan (default options)
Step‑4: Click the Scan button to start scanning.
Step‑5: Observe the progress in the Nmap Output tab.
Step‑6: Once the scan completes, explore the results in different tabs:

Ports/Hosts – open ports and services
Topology – traceroute to the target
Host Details – summary of the target
Scans – command history
Part C: Using Nmap from Command Line
Step‑1: Open Command Prompt (Windows) or Terminal (Linux/macOS).
Step‑2: Basic scan syntax:

bash
v1.0
nmap <target>
Step‑3: Common scan commands:

Command	Description
nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24	Ping scan (discover live hosts)
nmap -sS 192.168.1.10	TCP SYN stealth scan
nmap -sT 192.168.1.10	TCP Connect scan
nmap -sU 192.168.1.10	UDP scan
nmap -sV 192.168.1.10	Version detection
nmap -O 192.168.1.10	OS detection
nmap -A 192.168.1.10	Aggressive scan (OS, version, scripts, traceroute)
nmap -p 22,80,443 192.168.1.10	Scan specific ports
nmap -p- 192.168.1.10	Scan all ports (1-65535)
nmap --script=vuln 192.168.1.10	Vulnerability scan using NSE scripts
Step‑4: View help:

bash
v1.0
nmap -h
Part D: Interpreting Results
Step‑1: In the Nmap Output tab, review the scan summary:

Number of hosts up/down
Open ports and services
OS detection results
Step‑2: In the Ports/Hosts tab:

List all open ports
View the service running on each port
Step‑3: In the Topology tab:

View the traceroute path to the target
See the number of hops
Step‑4: In the Host Details tab:

View a summary of the target:
IP address
Hostname
Operating system
Open ports count
Part E: Saving Scan Results
Step‑1: In Zenmap, go to File → Save Scan to save results in .xml or .txt format.
Step‑2: In the command line, use output options:

bash
v1.0
nmap -oN output.txt <target>    # Normal output
nmap -oX output.xml <target>    # XML output
nmap -oG output.gnmap <target>  # Grepable output
